# Service & Repository Patterns Guidelines

## Scope / Flexibility Notice
These guidelines define patterns for implementing services and repositories across the Flutter Chat App. They ensure consistent data access, caching strategies, and separation between data sources and business logic. These guidelines apply to all data handling components but allow for adaptations based on specific feature requirements.

## Best Practices

### Repository Pattern
- Implement the Repository Pattern to abstract data sources from business logic
- Define repository interfaces in the domain layer
- Create implementations in the data layer
- Use repositories to coordinate between remote and local data sources
- Apply the Offline-First approach through repositories
- Handle data transformations between domain entities and data models
- Include proper error handling and recovery mechanisms
- Implement retry logic for network operations where appropriate
- Cache results to improve performance and reduce network calls
- Use typed failures instead of exceptions for predictable error handling

### Service Pattern
- Create services for cross-cutting concerns and shared functionality
- Inject dependencies rather than creating them inside services
- Keep services focused on a single responsibility
- Make services stateless when possible
- Use dependency injection to provide services throughout the app
- Document public methods and parameters clearly
- Implement proper cleanup in disposable services
- Handle async initialization properly
- Log service errors for diagnosis
- Make services testable through abstractions

## File / Project Structure Guidelines

### Repository Structure
```
lib/
  ├── domain/
  │   └── repositories/
  │       └── message_repository.dart      # Repository interface
  └── data/
      ├── repositories/
      │   └── message_repository_impl.dart  # Repository implementation
      └── datasources/
          ├── remote/
          │   └── message_remote_datasource.dart
          └── local/
              └── message_local_datasource.dart
```

### Service Structure
```
lib/
  └── core/
      ├── services/
      │   ├── connectivity_service.dart
      │   ├── localization_service.dart
      │   └── authentication_service.dart
      └── interfaces/
          └── storage_interface.dart
```

## Coding & Styling Conventions

### Repositories
- Name repository interfaces without suffix: `MessageRepository`
- Name implementations with `Impl` suffix: `MessageRepositoryImpl`
- Return domain entities from repository methods, not data models
- Use `Either<Failure, T>` return type for operations that can fail
- Use meaningful method names that describe the operation
- Include timeout parameters for network operations
- Handle pagination consistently
- Document complex methods with examples

### Services
- Name services with the `Service` suffix
- Use verbs for method names (e.g., `authenticate`, `fetchData`)
- Return strongly typed results, not dynamic objects
- Handle initialization in the constructor or dedicated init method
- Implement proper state management for stateful services
- Dispose resources properly when services are no longer needed

### Example Repository Interface
```dart
abstract class MessageRepository {
  /// Gets a message by its ID
  /// 
  /// Returns a [Message] entity if found, or a [Failure] if an error occurs
  Future<Either<Failure, Message>> getMessageById(String id);
  
  /// Sends a new message
  /// 
  /// Returns the sent [Message] with server ID, or a [Failure] if sending fails
  Future<Either<Failure, Message>> sendMessage(Message message);
  
  /// Gets a stream of messages for a specific chat
  /// 
  /// The stream emits an updated list whenever messages change
  Stream<List<Message>> getMessagesForChat(String chatId);
}
```

### Example Repository Implementation
```dart
class MessageRepositoryImpl implements MessageRepository {
  final MessageRemoteDataSource remoteDataSource;
  final MessageLocalDataSource localDataSource;
  final NetworkInfo networkInfo;

  MessageRepositoryImpl({
    required this.remoteDataSource,
    required this.localDataSource,
    required this.networkInfo,
  });

  @override
  Future<Either<Failure, Message>> getMessageById(String id) async {
    try {
      // Try to get from local cache first
      final localMessage = await localDataSource.getMessageById(id);
      
      // Check if we need to refresh from remote
      if (networkInfo.isConnected && _needsRefresh(localMessage)) {
        try {
          final remoteMessage = await remoteDataSource.getMessageById(id);
          await localDataSource.cacheMessage(remoteMessage);
          return Right(remoteMessage.toDomain());
        } on ServerException {
          // If remote fails, return cached data with warning
          return Right(localMessage.toDomain());
        }
      }
      
      return Right(localMessage.toDomain());
    } on CacheException {
      // If no cached data, try remote if connected
      if (await networkInfo.isConnected) {
        try {
          final remoteMessage = await remoteDataSource.getMessageById(id);
          await localDataSource.cacheMessage(remoteMessage);
          return Right(remoteMessage.toDomain());
        } on ServerException {
          return Left(ServerFailure());
        }
      }
      return Left(CacheFailure());
    }
  }
  
  @override
  Future<Either<Failure, Message>> sendMessage(Message message) async {
    if (await networkInfo.isConnected) {
      try {
        // Save to local storage with pending status
        final messageModel = MessageModel.fromDomain(message, status: MessageStatus.sending);
        await localDataSource.cacheMessage(messageModel);
        
        // Send to server
        final remoteSavedMessage = await remoteDataSource.sendMessage(messageModel);
        
        // Update local cache with server response
        await localDataSource.cacheMessage(remoteSavedMessage);
        
        return Right(remoteSavedMessage.toDomain());
      } on ServerException {
        // Mark as failed in local storage
        final failedMessageModel = MessageModel.fromDomain(message, status: MessageStatus.failed);
        await localDataSource.cacheMessage(failedMessageModel);
        
        return Left(ServerFailure());
      }
    } else {
      // Save locally for later sync when offline
      final offlineMessageModel = MessageModel.fromDomain(message, status: MessageStatus.pending);
      await localDataSource.cacheMessage(offlineMessageModel);
      
      // Queue for sync when online
      await localDataSource.queueMessageForSync(offlineMessageModel);
      
      return Right(message);
    }
  }
  
  @override
  Stream<List<Message>> getMessagesForChat(String chatId) {
    return localDataSource.getMessagesForChatStream(chatId)
      .map((models) => models.map((model) => model.toDomain()).toList());
  }
  
  bool _needsRefresh(MessageModel message) {
    final lastRefresh = message.lastRefreshed ?? DateTime.fromMillisecondsSinceEpoch(0);
    final refreshThreshold = const Duration(minutes: 5);
    return DateTime.now().difference(lastRefresh) > refreshThreshold;
  }
}
```

### Example Service
```dart
@lazySingleton
class ConnectivityService {
  final Connectivity _connectivity;
  final StreamController<bool> _connectionStatusController;
  StreamSubscription<ConnectivityResult>? _subscription;
  
  ConnectivityService(this._connectivity)
      : _connectionStatusController = StreamController<bool>.broadcast() {
    _initialize();
  }
  
  /// Initialize the service and start listening to connectivity changes
  Future<void> _initialize() async {
    // Set initial value
    _connectionStatusController.add(await isConnected());
    
    // Listen for changes
    _subscription = _connectivity.onConnectivityChanged.listen((result) {
      _connectionStatusController.add(result != ConnectivityResult.none);
    });
  }
  
  /// Stream of connectivity status changes (true = connected, false = disconnected)
  Stream<bool> get connectionStatusStream => _connectionStatusController.stream;
  
  /// Check current connectivity status
  Future<bool> isConnected() async {
    final result = await _connectivity.checkConnectivity();
    return result != ConnectivityResult.none;
  }
  
  /// Dispose resources
  void dispose() {
    _subscription?.cancel();
    _connectionStatusController.close();
  }
}
```

## Performance & Optimization Tips
- Cache repository results for frequently accessed data
- Use batched updates to reduce database operations
- Implement connection pooling for remote data sources
- Apply lazy loading for large data sets
- Use optimistic updates for better perceived performance
- Schedule background syncs for non-critical updates
- Implement query optimization for database operations
- Use indexing for frequently queried fields
- Monitor and optimize network request sizes
- Cache transformed entities to avoid redundant mapping
- Debounce rapid API calls to prevent overloading
- Use appropriate isolation for CPU-intensive operations

## Testing & Validation Guidelines
- Mock external dependencies for unit testing
- Test repository implementations with fake data sources
- Verify offline behavior works as expected
- Test error handling and recovery scenarios
- Include tests for edge cases and boundary conditions
- Validate caching behavior and expiration logic
- Test concurrency scenarios for race conditions
- Verify retry logic works correctly
- Include integration tests for repository and data source interaction
- Test services in isolation from their dependencies
- Verify proper cleanup and resource disposal
- Test initialization and error recovery
